UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 2: Movement circuits', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on the spot, skipping, hopping, balancing and jumping. Circuits work best with a clear signal to start and stop, a rotation direction, enough space between stations and an easier or harder option for each person. Rest and water breaks keep the body safe.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA circuit has a crowded station where two people bump into each other. Suggest two changes that make the circuit safer and fairer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd more space or markers, allow only two people per station and use one rotation direction with a clear stop signal. These changes prevent collisions and give every person a turn.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving the fastest person at a circuit has done it best. Success is working safely with good technique at your own level.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign a six-station circuit for your household with timing, a rotation direction, one safety rule and an easier and a harder choice at every station. Try it, record your effort from 1 to 5 at each station and suggest one improvement.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on the spot, skipping, hopping, balancing and jumping. Circuits work best with a clear signal to start and stop, a rotation direction, enough space between stations and an easier or harder option for each person. Rest and water breaks keep the body safe.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design a six-station circuit for your household with timing, a rotation direction, one safety rule and an easier and a harder choice at every station. Try it, record your effort from 1 to 5 at each station and suggest on Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a circuit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best.. Correction: Add more space or markers, allow only two people per station and use one rotation direction with a clear stop signal.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best. Success is working safely with good technique at your own level. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a circuit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a circuit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking 