 lesson idea about a circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a station and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Design a six-station circuit for your household with timing, a rotation direction, one safety rule and an easier and a","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a circuit to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a circuit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a circuit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a circuit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a circuit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a circuit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best.. Correction: Add more space or markers, allow only two people per station and use one rotation direction with a clear stop signal.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w06-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a circuit.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use a circuit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a station and explain why it matters.","I can practise a signal independently on a new prompt.","I can notice your own level needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on the spot, skipping, hopping, balancing and jumping.","options":["A movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each foot, 4 balance on one foot, 5 jump to a cone. Each station has an easier choice (march) and a harder one (high knees). Signal: a clap. Water break after station 5.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA circuit has a crowded station where two people bump into each other. Suggest two changes that make the circuit safer and fairer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd more space or markers, allow only two people per station and use one rotation direction with a clear stop signal. These changes prevent collisions and give every person a turn.","explain":"A movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on the spot, skipping, hopping, balancing and jumping. Circuits work best with a clear signal to start and stop, a rotation direction, enough space between stations and an easier or harder option for each person. Rest and water breaks keep the body safe.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best. Success is working safely with good technique at your own level. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A five-station circuit.","caption":"Rotate on a signal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stations</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">jog, skip, hop, balance, jump</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Signal</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clap to start and rotate</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each foot, 4 balance on one foot, 5 jump to a cone. Each station has an easier choice (march) and a harder one (high knees). Signal: a clap. Water break after station 5. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Choices at a station.","caption":"Choose your level.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Easier</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">march</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Harder</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">high knees</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving the fastest person at a circuit has done it best. Success is working safely with good technique at your own level. Here is a more helpful example: Example circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each foot, 4 balance on one foot, 5 jump to a cone. Each station has an easier choice (march) and a harder one (high knees). Signal: a clap. Water break after station 5.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd more space or markers, allow only two people per station and use one rotation direction with a clear stop signal. These changes prevent collisions and give every person a turn.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing the fastest person at a circuit has done it best. Success is working safely with good technique at your own level. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a circuit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA movement circuit is a set of stations, each practising a skill for a short time before rotating, such as jogging on the spot, skipping, hopping, balancing and jumping. Circuits work best with a clear signal to start and stop, a rotation direction, enough space between stations and an easier or harder option for each person. Rest and water breaks keep the body safe. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 2: Movement circuits","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Fundamental movement skills","week":6,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample circuit with 5 stations, 40 seconds each and 20 seconds to rotate: 1 jog on the spot, 2 skipping, 3 hop on each foot, 4 balance on one foot, 5 jump to a cone. Each station has an easier choice (march) and a harder one (high knees). Signal: a clap. Water break after station 5.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A five-station circuit.","caption":"Rotate on a signal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stations</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">jog, skip, hop, balance, jump</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Signal</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clap to start and rotate</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Choices at a station.","caption":"Choose your level.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\